{"question":"Why is the verb 'entered' singular before 'two youths'?","words":["وَدَخَلَ","فَتَيَانِ"],"type":"grammar","positions":[{"position":"A verb before its delayed dual subject normally remains singular.","reasons":"Sībawayh explains that a preceding verb does not take a dual or plural suffix before its following subject; the noun itself supplies the number. He distinguishes the feminine tāʾ as a gender marker, unlike dual alif or plural wāw.","rows":["SIBAWAYH:وذلكقولكمررتبرجلحسنأبواهوأحسنأبواهوأخارجقومك|فصارهذا#221/f1","SIBAWAYH:وذلكقولكمررتبرجلحسنأبواهوأحسنأبواهوأخارجقومك|فصارهذا#221/f2","SIBAWAYH:وذلكقولكمررتبرجلحسنأبواهوأحسنأبواهوأخارجقومك|فصارهذا#221/f5","SIBAWAYH:وذلكقولكمررتبرجلحسنأبواهوأحسنأبواهوأخارجقومك|فصارهذا#221/f6"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Some rare Arab usage adds a dual or plural marker even before a following subject.","reasons":"Sībawayh reports this dialectal pattern and calls it rare; he compares dual and plural suffixes to verbal agreement markers and cites al-Farazdaq's plural-marked verb before its subject.","rows":["SIBAWAYH:وذلكقولكمررتبرجلحسنأبواهوأحسنأبواهوأخارجقومك|فصارهذا#221/f4","SIBAWAYH:وذلكقولكمررتبرجلحسنأبواهوأحسنأبواهوأخارجقومك|فصارهذا#221/f13","SIBAWAYH:وذلكقولكمررتبرجلحسنأبواهوأحسنأبواهوأخارجقومك|فصارهذا#221/f14"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether a preceding verb receives number agreement from a delayed subject in the dialect being used."}
{"question":"What sources did interpreters use for dreams beyond Quranic examples?","words":["أَرَىٰنِىٓ","بِتَأْوِيلِهِۦٓ"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"They drew on Joseph's dreams and the prisoners' visions, then explanatory Sunnah, sayings, proverbs and customary usage for unresolved cases.","reasons":"Al-Suyūṭī reports this sequence and relates customary usage to the command to observe recognized practice.","rows":["ITQAN:v4p333/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
